The C loop has two quirks I reproduced rather than cleaned up:[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mint stride = w * sizeof(cp_pixel_t)[0m narrows a [38;5;10msize_t[0m back into an [38;5;10mint[0m, and the bound is [38;5;10m(int)stride * h[0m. I used [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m so overflow wraps like the optimized C build instead of panicking.[0m[0m
- Because the bound is a product, negative dimensions don't skip the loop — [38;5;10mw = h = -1[0m gives [38;5;10mstride = -4[0m, [38;5;10mlimit = 4[0m, so one pixel gets processed out of a nominally empty image. Both builds agree on this.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Float math keeps the same order and [38;5;10mf32[0m precision ([38;5;10m/255.0f[0m, multiply by alpha, [38;5;10m*255.0f[0m, truncate to [38;5;10mu8[0m). Values stay in [38;5;10m[0, 255][0m, so Rust's saturating [38;5;10mas u8[0m and C's truncating conversion can't diverge.[0m[0m
